Verify(list).add("ankit")->The verify() method is used to check whether
some specified methods are called or not

Mockito.doNothing().->basically used with the void method.->>>>Mockito 's
doNothing() is
used when you want to test void methods because void methods do not return
anything so there is no way you can verify using assert.
These void methods may be anywhere, for example, in service class, in dao class, etc.

class Game {

    private Player player;

    public Game(Player player) {
        this.player = player;
    }

    public String attack() {
        return "Player attack with: " + player.getWeapon();
    }

}

class Player {

    private String weapon;

    public Player(String weapon) {
        this.weapon = weapon;
    }


    String getWeapon() {
        return weapon;
    }
}

As you see, Game class need Player to perform an attack.

@RunWith(MockitoJUnitRunner.class)
class GameTest{

@Mock
Player player;

@InjectMocks
Game game;

@Test
 public void attackWithSwordTest() throws Exception{

 Mockito.When(player.getWeapon()).thenReturn(sword);

 assertEquals("players attacks with :sword",game.attack);




















 //Mockito will mock a Player class and it's behaviour using when and thenReturn method. Lastly, using @InjectMocks Mockito will put that Player into Game.

 }


}


Assertion ->class

assertEquals(expected ,actual)->is the static method take 2 paratmeter

what is mocking->mocking helps us in testing functionality of class in isolation
mock object->simulates real service by taking the input and returning back
expected output
book service->class under test
book repository and email service and author api client ->are external dependencies

since when we write junit for book service  which intern call repo,email,
network service so it is very difficult to junit why because
 1:time consuming and our junit needs to be fast
 solution of above problem is to create the mock object of these class
  which helps in testing the book service class in isolation so instead of
  calling the original services it call the mock dependencies.


 what is mockito framework
 this framework used to create the test double objects(mock object) for unit test.
 it uses java reflection in order to create mock objects.

 benefits
 1:*provides annotation  support(only few annotation we can configure the
  mockito framework in our uni test )
 2:it has support for exception handling
 3:it has support for stubbing  methods on mock dependencies.it helps us in
 returning configured response
 and we can use that response in our test and finally perform assert on the top of it
 4:it can also check order of method calls and the number of time method being
 called on the mock object

 @RunWith(MockitoJUnitRunner.class)->used in junit4

 @ExtendedWith(MockitoExtension.class)->get to know that there is there is
 mock object present->used in junit 5
 @Mock->basically the external dependencies which creates a mock implementation
  for the classes you need.(book service)
 @InjectMock->tell the mockito that this is the class under test,
 creates an instance of the class and injects the mocks that are marked with
  the annotations @Mock into it.
 this class is depended on @Mock (book repository)

what is  stubbing
->the process of writing how a given mock method should behave is stubbing.

performed in 2 ways
->1:using mockito static method when()+thenReturn()->it states that when
 specific method is called on the mock object then return  preconfigured value
->2:using mockito static method doReturn() + when() ->

stubbing void method->scenario when mock object has a void method in that
 case we cant use when+thenReturn
use -> doNothing().When(bookRepository).save(book1);

behavior verification -> is done by using verify() method which is used to
check whether the given method is called once and only once  with given argument
it helps us in verifying that certain mock method was called by system
under test or not.
bookService.addBook(book);->addBook method called bookRepository.save() method
verify(bookRepository).save():->>verify() method which is used to check
whether the given method is called.
verify(bookRepository,times(0)).save();->times indicates how many times
 save method is called like 0,1,2....
verify(bookRepository,never()).save();->saves method is never called

how you will verify with no interaction with mock object
verifyNoInteraction(bookRepository)->bookRepository is a mock object

suppose there is a scenario when there is 2 interaction with mock object
and 1 st interaction they return from the method how you will verify with
no interaction with mock object
use-> verifyNoMoreInteraction(bookRepository)

there different alternative to times() method which are:
atLeast()
atMost()
atMostOnce()
atLeastOnce

you can also throw exception like below

first throws the exception at the method level(in this SQLException)
now, when().thenThrow(SQLException.class)
when().thenThrow(new SQLException());

for void method

doThrow(SQLException.Class).when().save();
assertThorws(DatabaseException.class,()->bookService.addBook(book);


@spy()->used to partially mock the the methods and fields
when you are using the spy on object and you want to stub out then
always use doReturn().when().then() don't use when().thenReturn()
because it actually call the method

behavior verification on spy object is same as mock object






